## What I did

The task is the Prefab "update many aggregates from one event" pattern. I wired it up on the existing model without touching any generated code:

**1. Event handler on `PriceWatch`** (`src/main/java/com/example/watch/PriceWatch.java`)

Added an instance handler that Prefab turns into a Kafka consumer for the `prices` topic:

```java
@EventHandler
@Multicast(queryMethod = "findByProduct", parameters = "product", failWhenEmpty = false)
public PriceWatch onPriceChanged(PriceChanged event) {
    return new PriceWatch(id, version, email, product, event.price());
}
```

- `@Multicast` makes the handler run against **every** `PriceWatch` whose `product` matches the event's `product` (a reference), so all watches of a product are updated to the new price in one go.
- `failWhenEmpty = false` is the key for the "no watches" requirement: when a product has no watches, Prefab's generated service sees an empty result and **succeeds** instead of throwing (which would cause retry / dead-lettering). I confirmed this in the generated `PriceWatchService`:

```java
var aggregates = priceWatchRepository.findByProduct(event.product());
if (aggregates.isEmpty()) { return; }
```

**2. Repository mixin** (`src/main/java/com/example/watch/PriceWatchRepositoryMixin.java`)

The `queryMethod` needs a Spring Data derived finder:

```java
@RepositoryMixin(PriceWatch.class)
public interface PriceWatchRepositoryMixin {
    List<PriceWatch> findByProduct(Reference<Product> product);
}
```

**3. Integration test** (`src/test/java/com/example/watch/PriceWatchIntegrationTest.java`) — `@IntegrationTest` driving the HTTP flow through the generated `ProductClient`/`PriceWatchClient`:
- two watches on one product both pick up the new price,
- a watch on a *different* product is left unchanged,
- a product with no watches updates without failing.

`GET /price-watches/{id}` already exposed `currentPrice` (via the generated response), so no change was needed there.

## Verification

- `mvn -DskipTests compile` — model and generated code compile.
- `mvn -Dtest=PriceWatchIntegrationTest test` — both new tests pass.
- `mvn verify` — full suite (including the generated `PriceChangedEventConformanceTest`) passes, `BUILD SUCCESS`.

Done. The build is green and all requirements are met.

## What I changed

**`OrderPlaced.java`** (new event record) — `com.example.ordering.OrderPlaced` with exactly the requested components (`order` as `Reference<Order>`, `customer` as `String`, `total` as `BigDecimal`):
```java
@Event(topic = "orders", platform = Event.Platform.KAFKA, serialization = Event.Serialization.JSON)
public record OrderPlaced(Reference<Order> order, String customer, BigDecimal total) {
    @PartitioningKey
    public String partitioningKey() { return order.id(); }
}
```
- **JSON payload** → `serialization = Event.Serialization.JSON`.
- **Kafka topic `orders`** → `topic = "orders"`, `platform = KAFKA`.
- **Partition by the order** → a `@PartitioningKey` method returning `order.id()`, so every event for the same order maps to the same key and therefore the same partition.

**`Order.java`** — publishes the event in the `@Create` constructor, which runs on `POST /orders`:
```java
private static final EventPublisher<OrderPlaced> P = EventPublisher.bind(OrderPlaced.class);
@Create
public Order(@NotNull String customer, @NotNull BigDecimal total) {
    this(Reference.create(), 0L, customer, total);
    P.publish(new OrderPlaced(id(), customer, total));
}
```

## Verification

I confirmed the wiring in the **generated** code (not by hand): the processor produced `OrderPlacedEventTypeRegistrar` registering exactly
`registry.register("orders", OrderPlaced.class, Event.Serialization.JSON, event -> event.partitioningKey())` — topic, JSON serialization, and partition key all correct. `target/prefab/agent-context.md` now lists "OrderPlaced on topic orders".

I added two tests:
- `OrderIntegrationTest` — creates an order through the generated `OrderClient` (`POST /orders`) and asserts an `OrderPlaced` event lands on `orders` with the right customer, total, and order reference.
- `OrderPlacedTest` — asserts the partition key equals `order.id()` and that two different orders get different keys (same order → same key → same partition).

`mvn clean verify` passes: 4 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS (includes the generated JSON-serialization conformance test for the event).

Done. The two queries are now part of the single generated `OrderRepository`.

## What I did

Prefab generates `com.example.ordering.application.OrderRepository` from the `Order` aggregate, and the cheat sheet's recipe for "a custom query" is a `@RepositoryMixin(Aggregate.class)` interface. I added one:

**`src/main/java/com/example/ordering/application/OrderRepositoryMixin.java`**
```java
@RepositoryMixin(Order.class)
public interface OrderRepositoryMixin {
    List<Order> findByCustomerEmail(String customerEmail);
    long countByStatus(OrderStatus status);
}
```

How this satisfies "add them to `OrderRepository`, do not create a second repository": `PersistenceWriter` adds every `@RepositoryMixin(Order.class)` interface as a superinterface of the generated repository. After the build, the generated interface is now:
```java
public interface OrderRepository extends CrudRepository<Order, String>,
        PagingAndSortingRepository<Order, String>, OrderRepositoryMixin { }
```
So the two methods live **on** the one generated `OrderRepository` that Spring Data instantiates — other beans `@Autowired` it and call `findByCustomerEmail`/`countByStatus` directly. The mixin is a merge point, not a second repository (no `@Repository`, doesn't extend `CrudRepository`, no bean is created for it). Both are Spring Data derived queries (`findBy…` → `WHERE customerEmail = ?`, `countBy…` → `COUNT ... WHERE status = ?`), so no `@Query` was needed.

**`src/test/java/com/example/ordering/OrderRepositoryQueryTest.java`** — a focused `@IntegrationTest` that creates orders via the generated `OrderClient`, then verifies both methods through the injected `OrderRepository` (per-customer listing incl. empty match, and per-status counts after a `ship()`).

## Verification
- `mvn clean verify` → exit `0`.
- `OrderRepositoryQueryTest`: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.
- Confirmed no hand-written/second order repository exists in `src`.

One note: Prefab printed `Skipping generation of … treated as a manual override` for a few test helpers after a *second* compile without clean — that's stale `target/prefab-test-sources` from a prior run, not an error; `mvn clean` regenerates them fresh, which the final `clean verify` confirms.

Done. The book catalogue is implemented, compiles, and the full test suite passes (`mvn verify` → BUILD SUCCESS).

## What I added

**`src/main/java/com/example/catalog/Book.java`** — a Prefab aggregate (no hand-written controllers, services, repositories, entities, or migrations — Prefab generates all of those):

```java
@Aggregate
@GetById
public record Book(
        @Id Reference<Book> id,
        @Version long version,
        String title, String author, String isbn, int publicationYear
) {
    @Create
    public Book(
            @NotBlank @Size(max = 200) String title,
            @NotBlank String author,
            @NotBlank String isbn,
            int publicationYear) {
        this(Reference.create(), 0L, title, author, isbn, publicationYear);
    }
}
```

Validation lives on the `@Create` parameters (Prefab ignores it on record components), giving required title/author/isbn and a whole-number `publicationYear`, with the 200-char limit on the title.

**`src/test/java/com/example/catalog/BookIntegrationTest.java`** — a `@IntegrationTest` driving the real HTTP layer via MockMvc: create → `201` with a `Location` header ending in the new id, then `GET /books/{id}` returns the book's fields; a missing id returns `404`.

## What Prefab generated from it (verified, never hand-edited)

- Endpoints: `POST /books` (201 + `Location: /books/{id}`) and `GET /books/{id}` (book JSON or 404) — confirmed in `target/generated-sources/.../BookController.java`.
- `BookResponse` exposes `id, title, author, isbn, publicationYear` (plus `version`); `Reference` serializes its `id` via `@JsonValue`, so `id` is a plain string.
- Flyway `V1__generated.sql` creates the `book` table — `id`, `version`, `title`, `author`, `isbn`, `publication_year` — persisted by Spring Data JDBC through `prefab-postgres`.

The starter's `pom.xml` already declared `prefab-postgres`, so books persist to PostgreSQL with no further config needed.

One note: the `java.io.IOException: Unable to create temporary directory .../surefire-stijnvanbael` lines in the log are the OS sandbox blocking a temp dir Surefire tries to make — the tests still ran and passed (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, BUILD SUCCESS).
